OWASP Top 10 (2025) — Updated January 2026

Two NEW categories added in 2025 vs 2021: A03 and A10.
Most freshers still quote 2021 — knowing 2025 makes you stand out in interviews.


A01:2025 — Broken Access Control
What it is: Users can access data or actions they are not supposed to.
Example:
Your profile URL: mybank.com/profile?user_id=1001
You change to:    mybank.com/profile?user_id=1002
→ You can see another person's account
India context: Found in Indian government portals — changing ID in URL exposed other citizens' Aadhaar-linked data.
Fix: Server-side authorization checks on every request.

A02:2025 — Security Misconfiguration
What it is: Default settings left unchanged, exposed admin panels, error messages revealing too much info.
Example:
Tomcat Manager exposed at /manager/html
Default credentials admin:admin still active
→ Attacker deploys malicious WAR file → Remote Code Execution
My VIT-AP report connection: Tomcat Manager, Host Manager, and Server Status pages were all publicly exposed — this is A02.
Fix: Disable unused features, change default credentials, restrict admin panels by IP.

A03:2025 — Software Supply Chain Failures ⭐ NEW
What it is: Risks introduced by third-party libraries, build tools, and CI/CD pipelines.
Example:
Developer uses popular npm package
Attacker compromises that package
Every app using it gets infected automatically
→ Developer did nothing wrong but their app is compromised
Famous case: SolarWinds attack — 18,000 organizations infected through a compromised software update.
Fix: Verify package integrity, use lock files, scan dependencies regularly.

A04:2025 — Cryptographic Failures
What it is: Weak or missing encryption — HTTP instead of HTTPS, weak password hashing.
Example:
Password stored as MD5: 5f4dcc3b5aa765d61d8327deb882cf99
→ Cracks in seconds at crackstation.net

Website uses HTTP on login page
→ Anyone on same WiFi can see username + password in Wireshark
Fix: Use HTTPS everywhere, bcrypt for passwords, TLS 1.3.

A05:2025 — Injection
What it is: User input gets executed as code by the server.
SQL Injection example:
sqlNormal:   SELECT * FROM users WHERE username='mani' AND password='pass'
Attack:   username = admin' --
Result:   SELECT * FROM users WHERE username='admin' --' AND password='pass'
          Password check skipped → logged in as admin
XSS example:
html<script>alert('XSS')</script>
→ Executes in victim's browser, can steal cookies
Command Injection example:
Input: 8.8.8.8; cat /etc/passwd
→ Server runs ping AND dumps all user accounts
Fix: Parameterized queries for SQLi, output encoding for XSS.

A06:2025 — Insecure Design
What it is: The flaw is in the design/architecture, not the code.
Example:
Forgot Password sends actual password in email
→ Means passwords stored in plain text
→ Entire feature needs to be redesigned from scratch
OTP example:
4-digit OTP with no lockout
Attacker tries all 10,000 combinations automatically
→ Design failure, not a coding bug
Fix: Threat modeling during design phase, rate limiting, account lockout.

A07:2025 — Authentication Failures
What it is: Weak login systems — easy to brute force, bypass, or steal sessions.
Example:
No account lockout → attacker tries millions of passwords
Weak session token: session_id=12345 → guess 12346 = next user's account
No MFA on admin → password leak = instant account takeover
Fix: MFA, account lockout after 5 attempts, strong random session tokens.

A08:2025 — Data Integrity Failures
What it is: Application trusts data it receives without verifying it hasn't been tampered with.
Example:
HTTP request: item=laptop&price=50000
Intercepted with Burp Suite → changed to: item=laptop&price=1
Server doesn't verify price from its own database → laptop bought for ₹1
Fix: Always verify critical values server-side, use digital signatures for updates.

A09:2025 — Security Logging Failures
What it is: No logs = attacks go undetected.
Example:
Attacker tries 50,000 login attempts over 3 days
No logs → no alerts → nobody notices
Attacker cracks account → stays inside for months undetected
Average attacker dwell time in enterprise: 200+ days
Blue team connection: SOC analysts use SIEM tools like Splunk to detect these patterns in logs.
Fix: Log all authentication events, failed logins, admin actions. Alert on anomalies.

A10:2025 — Mishandling of Exceptional Conditions ⭐ NEW
What it is: App crashes on unexpected input and reveals sensitive information.
Example:
Input: Age = "abc" (letter instead of number)
App crashes and shows:
TypeError at /users/profile
Database: mysql://admin:password123@localhost/users
File: /var/www/html/profile.php line 34
→ Exposed DB password and internal file structure from one error
Fix: Generic error messages to users, detailed errors only in server logs.

